\caption(The accumulator values 0,1,3,6,13 are captured immediately after each actual shift. The final character is emitted before that accumulator and the five-bit group counter reset.)
\alttext(Three boxes link point 42.6,-5.6 to actual bits 01101/value 13 and emitted e with longitude minus 45 to 0 and latitude 0 to 45.)

> A prefix identifies a cell, not an exact point or a distance

At each step the active interval is bisected using (lo+hi)/2 in double arithmetic.
Equality chooses the upper half. Grouping the decisions by five bits uses the
alphabet shown above; a shorter encoding of the same point is a prefix of its
longer encoding. Longitude and latitude decisions are interleaved most-significant
bit first. This is the rectangular grid’s Morton/Z bit ordering, written in base32,
not a separate alternative to Morton ordering.

[Chris Veness’s primary encoding/decoding implementation](https://www.movable-type.co.uk/scripts/geohash.html)
and its [source](https://github.com/chrisveness/latlon-geohash/blob/master/latlon-geohash.js)
provide the longitude-first midpoint/alphabet and bounds-decoding reference.
This library preserves its own legacy required precision and double operation
order. Coordinates must be finite within latitude[−90,90] and longitude [−180,180].
The new decoder is strict lowercase, treats an empty key as the world, and returns
an unrounded computed center. It does not lowercase input or decimal-round that
center. The two end-meridians are accepted distinctly rather than normalized.

When two keys share a prefix, both follow the same early subdivisions and lie
in the same prefix cell in the ideal grid. The converse fails: close points can
straddle an early split. A prefix is useful for hierarchical grouping, but it is
not a metric distance or a complete radius-search predicate. Floating endpoint
limits at very high character counts need an additional distinction, shown later.

> Reproduce the original coordinate and city examples

The original coordinate gives ezs42 at five characters. London and Greenwich
share the prefix gcp at seven characters, while Sydney begins on a different
branch. Their common prefix bounds come from native decoding. These particular
points illustrate one shared cell; they do not establish a universal implication
from geographical proximity to long prefix agreement.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hash_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/geohash.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto canonical=dm::algorithms::geohash_encode_run(42.6,-5.6,5);const auto cell=dm::algorithms::geohash_decode(canonical.hash);
 const std::array<std::string,3>names{"London","Greenwich","Sydney"};const std::array<std::array<double,2>,3>locations{{{51.5074,-.1278},{51.4779,-.0015},{-33.8688,151.2093}}};std::array<std::string,3>keys;
 std::print("Canonical key {}; decoded latitude [{:.8f}, {:.8f}]; longitude [{:.8f}, {:.8f}].\n\n",canonical.hash,cell.bounds.lat_lo,cell.bounds.lat_hi,cell.bounds.lon_lo,cell.bounds.lon_hi);
 std::print("| location | latitude | longitude | seven-character key |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
 for(std::size_t i=0;i<names.size();++i){keys[i]=dm::algorithms::geohash_encode(locations[i][0],locations[i][1],7);std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {} |\n",names[i],locations[i][0],locations[i][1],keys[i]);}
 const auto n=dm::algorithms::geohash_common_prefix(keys[0],keys[1]);const auto prefix=keys[0].substr(0,n);const auto common=dm::algorithms::geohash_decode(prefix);
 std::print("\nLondon/Greenwich common prefix {} ({} characters); London/Sydney shared characters {}; common longitude [{:.6f}, {:.6f}], latitude [{:.6f}, {:.6f}].\n",prefix,n,dm::algorithms::geohash_common_prefix(keys[0],keys[2]),common.bounds.lon_lo,common.bounds.lon_hi,common.bounds.lat_lo,common.bounds.lat_hi);
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(61,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4.5;box.height=2.2;box.font_size=22;
 d.node("london",{0,0},std::format("{}\n{}",names[0],keys[0]),box).node("greenwich",{6,0},std::format("{}\n{}",names[1],keys[1]),box)
  .node("sydney",{12,0},std::format("{}\n{}",names[2],keys[2]),box)
  .node("common",{3,-3.5},std::format("Common prefix: {}\nlon [{:.3f}, {:.3f}]\nlat [{:.3f}, {:.3f}]",prefix,common.bounds.lon_lo,common.bounds.lon_hi,common.bounds.lat_lo,common.bounds.lat_hi),box)
  .node("other",{12,-3.5},std::format("Different branch\nshared with London: {}",dm::algorithms::geohash_common_prefix(keys[0],keys[2])),box)
  .edge("london","common","prefix").edge("greenwich","common","prefix").edge("sydney","other","compare")
  .text({6,2.4},"Shared prefixes identify common angular cells",25).save_svg("build/hash_original.svg");
 if(canonical.hash!="ezs42"||prefix!="gcp"||n!=3)throw std::runtime_error("original canonical and city outputs");
}
