> Derive the angular resolution from the bit counts

After p characters there are 5p decisions. Starting with longitude gives

$$
b_\lambda=\lceil5p/2\rceil,\qquad b_\phi=\lfloor5p/2\rfloor,
\qquad \Delta\lambda=360\,2^{-b_\lambda},\qquad
\Delta\phi=180\,2^{-b_\phi}.
$$
\alttext(Longitude receives the ceiling of five p divided by two bits, latitude the floor; full ideal angular widths are360 and180 degrees divided by the corresponding powers of two.)

Every added character splits one parent into 32 angular subcells. Individual axes
alternate between two and three new decisions: longitude shrinks by 8 then 4 then 8,
while latitude shrinks by 4 then 8 then 4. Thus the angular area divides by 32 each
character, but neither side has a fixed per-character factor of about 5.7. Odd
character counts give square degree cells; even counts give longitude span twice
the latitude span. These are angular shapes, not equal metric widths on Earth.

The old chart multiplied longitude degrees by 111.32 km/degree as an approximate
equatorial scale. The new plot uses angular units directly, and the original
program and SVG remain separately verified for compatibility. In a local spherical
model the east-west length includes cos(latitude); a prefix length alone cannot
specify a universal kilometre radius. In particular, five-character full angular
width is 0.0439453125°, rather than a universally fixed 2.4 km side.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hash_resolution.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/geohash.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 std::vector<double>p,lon_y,lat_y;std::print("| characters | longitude bits | latitude bits | ideal longitude degrees | ideal latitude degrees | longitude shrink | latitude shrink |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(int n=1;n<=12;++n){const auto r=dm::algorithms::geohash_resolution(n),before=dm::algorithms::geohash_resolution(n-1);std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.12g} | {:.12g} | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |\n",n,r.longitude_bits,r.latitude_bits,r.longitude_degrees,r.latitude_degrees,before.longitude_degrees/r.longitude_degrees,before.latitude_degrees/r.latitude_degrees);p.push_back(n);lon_y.push_back(std::log2(r.longitude_degrees));lat_y.push_back(std::log2(r.latitude_degrees));}
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.lines(p,lon_y,"Longitude",{37,99,235},2).points(p,lon_y,"",{37,99,235},5)
  .lines(p,lat_y,"Latitude",{220,38,38},2).points(p,lat_y,"",{220,38,38},5).legend({"Longitude","Latitude"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
  .title("Ideal angular sides alternate factors of four and eight").xlab("Characters").ylab("log2 full angular span in degrees").y_limits(-25,10).size(1100,560).save_svg("build/hash_resolution.svg");
 if(dm::algorithms::geohash_resolution(5).longitude_degrees!=.0439453125)throw std::runtime_error("five-character full angular width");
}
