> Arbitrarily close points can have no shared first character

Take latitude 0 and longitudes±10^-6 degrees. The first longitude split is exactly
zero. One point goes west, the other east, so their first bits differ and they
share no prefix, however long the keys are. Reducing the separation keeps the
same argument, including representable values immediately either side of zero.
At the date line, longitude −180 and +180 describe the same physical meridian but
are assigned different edge cells by this inclusive, non-normalizing encoder.

A location search must cover the relevant cells across boundaries, then apply
its actual geographic predicate. The required covering set depends on the radius,
latitude and chosen cell size; checking exactly eight neighbours is not a universal
solution for arbitrary-radius queries. The plot is a split demonstration in
microdegrees, not a projection or a physical-distance map.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hash_boundary.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/geohash.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 constexpr double epsilon=1e-6;const auto west=dm::algorithms::geohash_encode(0,-epsilon,7),east=dm::algorithms::geohash_encode(0,epsilon,7);const auto shared=dm::algorithms::geohash_common_prefix(west,east);
 const auto dateline_w=dm::algorithms::geohash_encode(0,-180,7),dateline_e=dm::algorithms::geohash_encode(0,180,7);const auto split=dm::algorithms::geohash_encode_trace(0,epsilon,1,7).bits[0].midpoint;
 std::print("| point | latitude | longitude | key |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| Just west | 0 | {:.9f} | {} |\n| Just east | 0 | {:.9f} | {} |\n| West end-meridian | 0 | -180 | {} |\n| East end-meridian | 0 | 180 | {} |\n",-epsilon,west,epsilon,east,dateline_w,dateline_e);
 std::print("\nNear-zero longitude separation {:.6f} microdegrees; shared characters {}; end-meridian shared characters {}; first longitude split {:.0f} degrees.\n",2*epsilon*1e6,shared,dm::algorithms::geohash_common_prefix(dateline_w,dateline_e),split);
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();plot.points({-epsilon*1e6},{0},"West",{37,99,235},9).points({epsilon*1e6},{0},"East",{220,38,38},9)
  .lines({split*1e6,split*1e6},{-1.5,1.5},"First split",{71,85,105},2).legend({"West","East","First split"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38},{71,85,105}})
  .title("Two points across the first split share zero characters").xlab("Longitude in microdegrees").ylab("Latitude in microdegrees").x_limits(-2,2).y_limits(-2,2).size(900,650).save_svg("build/hash_boundary.svg");
 if(shared||dm::algorithms::geohash_common_prefix(dateline_w,dateline_e))throw std::runtime_error("prefix boundary counterexample");
}
